Q: Why can&#39;t I use required with private init and a private constructor in C#? A: <p>This isn't a &quot;best practices&quot; question. You'll find the &quot;why&quot; for C# design decisions at <a href="https://github.com/dotnet/csharplang" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/dotnet/csharplang</a> , the Github repo containing all proposals <em>and</em> the discussions around them. The <a href="https://github.com/dotnet/csharplang/issues/3630" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Required Properties proposal</a> is a GitHub issue that links to the discussion documents.</p>
<p>By the way, this snippet works, and ensures <code>Property</code> can only be set by the factory method.</p>
<pre><code>public class  Example
{
    public string Property { get;  private init; }

    private Example(string property) { Property=property;}

    public static Example Create(string property) =&gt; new Example( property );
}
</code></pre>
<p>The decision meeting seems to be <a href="https://github.com/dotnet/csharplang/blob/main/meetings/2022/LDM-2022-03-23.md" rel="nofollow noreferrer">LDM-2022-03-23.md</a> but there are many more that lead to this. The compiler must work on every case, and <code>init</code> etc were introduced to eventually enable <code>record</code>. If the compiler can't guarantee things will work, or if it takes too much work, a feature won't be implemented.</p>
<p>The wording is :</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Unsettable members</strong></p>
<p>We've previously said constructors that require members must be at least as accessible as the members, to ensure that constructors don't become uncallable. This is just an extension of that scenario.</p>
<p><strong>Conclusion</strong></p>
<p>We will require that required fields cannot be readonly, and that required properties are settable from every constructor (meaning that they have a setter/initer at least as accessible as every constructor that requires them to be set).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The example for unsettable members is this, which prevents the creation of the type.  :</p>
<pre><code>class C
{
    public required readonly int Field;
    public required int Prop1 { get; }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Static methods are <em>not</em> special methods when it comes to construction of a type. To have the compiler treat a factory method as special. BUT <code>required</code> doesn't affect construction, it's an added restriction on the object. Getting <code>required ... {; private set;}</code> to work would mean changing how construction works and marking at least some of those factory methods. So how would they be different from actual constructors?</p>
<p>To understand what's going on, you can look at the <a href="https://sharplab.io/#v2:CYLg1APgAgTAjAWAFBQMwAJboKIA8CGAtgA4A2ApsgN7Lp2YYBO5AjgK4CWzwmcADOgAKjAPbFyjAC4BPdFXQBzcpIDcdDgDsOq9AF9a9A3WKMOAN3yTyOAiQoAKAJRy9yIw14A2G0TLWAwsyW5PZQ/OgmYhIyzgC8AHzoGuQA7j521vLCUVKysRGi4rl6Ksi6QA" rel="nofollow noreferrer">actual generated C# code in SharpLab</a>.</p>
<p>This snippet:</p>
<pre><code>public class Example
{
    public required string Property { get;  init; }

    private Example() { }

    public static Example Create(string property) =&gt; new Example { Property = property };
}
</code></pre>
<p>Translates to :</p>
<pre><code>public class Example
{
    [CompilerGenerated]
    [DebuggerBrowsable(DebuggerBrowsableState.Never)]
    private readonly string &lt;Property&gt;k__BackingField;

    [RequiredMember]
    public string Property
    {
        [CompilerGenerated]
        get
        {
            return &lt;Property&gt;k__BackingField;
        }
        [CompilerGenerated]
        init
        {
            &lt;Property&gt;k__BackingField = value;
        }
    }

    [Obsolete(&quot;Constructors of types with required members are not supported in this version of your compiler.&quot;, true)]
    [CompilerFeatureRequired(&quot;RequiredMembers&quot;)]
    private Example()
    {
    }

    public static Example Create(string property)
    {
        Example example = new Example();
        example.Property = property;
        return example;
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: What is the proper usage of CollectionView(Source) in MVVM pattern? A: <p>As mentioned by <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/4102716/eder-cardoso">Eder Cardoso</a> CollectionViewSource it is technically acceptable to use in ViewModels, it ties the view models directly to WPF's presentation framework, but If you ever want to port this logic to .NET MAUI, etc. this will break.</p>
<p>An alternative I prefer is using an ObservableCollection to hold the the items, If I want to filter the data too, I'd use LINQ and filter it into a separate property, and bind only the filtered data to the view.</p>
<p>An example:<br />
In the ViewModel:</p>
<pre><code> private string _filterText = string.Empty;

 private readonly ObservableCollection&lt;ElementItemViewModel&gt; _elementsList;
 private ObservableCollection&lt;ElementItemViewModel&gt; _filteredElementsList;

 public ElementCategoriesExpanderViewModel(ElementListViewModel elementList)
 {
     _elementsList = elementList.ElementsList;
     _filteredElementsList = _elementsList;
 }


public ObservableCollection&lt;ElementItemViewModel&gt; FilteredElementsList
{
    get =&gt; _filteredElementsList;
    set =&gt; SetField(ref _filteredElementsList, value);
}

// in the view this is bound to a TextBox
public string FilterText
{
    get =&gt; _filterText;
    set
    {
        if (SetField(ref _filterText, value))
        {
            var filtered = string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_filterText)
                ? _elementsList
                : new ObservableCollection&lt;ElementItemViewModel&gt;(
                    _elementsList
                        .Where(e =&gt; !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(e.Name) &amp;&amp; e.Name.Contains(_filterText, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
                );
            FilteredElementsList = filtered;
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>in the view I have:</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>
           &lt;ItemsControl Margin=&quot;0,0,0,0&quot; 
                     Style=&quot;{StaticResource LauncherGridStyle}&quot;
                     ItemsSource=&quot;{Binding FilteredElementsList}&quot;
                     ItemTemplate=&quot;{StaticResource ElementContainerDataTemplate}&quot; /&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: Convert css code =&gt; transform: translate(-50%, -50%) to tailwindcss A: <p>Tailwind has <strong><a href="https://tailwindcss.com/docs/translate#basic-usage" rel="nofollow noreferrer">translate classes</a></strong>. To use negative values you just need to prefix the class with <code>-</code></p>
<p><code>-translate-x-1/2 -translate-y-1/2</code></p>
<p>This works because tailwind is setting CSS variables for both, like so:</p>
<pre><code>--tw-translate-x: -50%;
--tw-translate-y: -50%;
</code></pre>
<p>And always includes them in its actual <code>transform</code> property:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>transform: 
  translate(
    var(--tw-translate-x), 
    var(--tw-translate-y)) 
    rotate(var(--tw-rotate)) 
    skewX(var(--tw-skew-x)) 
    skewY(var(--tw-skew-y)) 
    scaleX(var(--tw-scale-x)) 
    scaleY(var(--tw-scale-y)
  );
</code></pre>
